--   **The adapter.**
--
--   The following holds (Lean statement, in the scope of module `Solutions.R6ILAd`):
--
--   ```lean
--   theorem IntMul.Kappa.R6.IL.ad_runs {I : List Sym} {tab : Fin 1 → IStmt HS 1} (m k M Bb : ℕ)
--       (hM : zsz (M : ℤ) ≤ Bb) (hkM : m ^ k ≤ M) (hmM : m ≤ M) (hm : 1 ≤ m) (hk : k ≤ M)
--       (t : IState HS) (R1 : List Item) (hJ : t.G dJ = .int (k : ℤ) :: R1) (X Y : List Item) (c0 : ℕ)
--       (hcall : ∀ t' : IState HS, t'.G dJ = .int (-1) :: t.G dJ →
--         t'.G dT = .int ((m ^ k : ℕ) : ℤ) :: t.G dT → (∀ j, j ≠ dJ → j ≠ dT → t'.G j = t.G j) →
--         IRuns I tab (tab 0) (t'.upd 0 X) (t'.upd 0 Y) c0) :
--       IRuns I tab (adS m) (t.upd 0 X) (t.upd 0 Y) (cAd m k Bb c0)
--   ```
--
--   **Context.** Round six, item language: the adapter from the KIL5 descriptor to the generic one
--
--   `adS m`: with `k` on top of `dJ`, compute `m ^ k` (loop on a scratch register), push it on `dT`,
--   push `-1` on `dJ`, call procedure `0`, pop `dJ` and `dT`. `ad_runs`: the adapter runs the call on
--   the state with the generic descriptor and restores every stack.
